               <msContents>
                  <summary>Sefer ha-Zikaron (Commentary on the Torah); and poetic works</summary>
                  <msItem n="1">
                     <locus>ff. 3r-212r</locus>
                     <title xml:lang="he" key="work_3378">ספר הזכרון : פרוש התורה</title>
                     <title xml:lang="he-Latn-x-lc" key="work_3378">Sefer ha-Zikaron: Perush
                        ha-Torah</title>
                     <author key="person_7038">
                        <persName>Elijah ben Samuel ben Parnes of Stephanow</persName>
                        <persName xml:lang="he">אליהו בן שמואל השטיפיוני</persName>
                     </author>
                     <incipit>
                        <foreign xml:lang="he">זה ספר הזכרון פשט התורה אשר חברתי אנכי אליהו הקטן
                           בכ׳׳ר שמואל הקטן בן פרנס נ׳׳ע השטיפיוני</foreign> (<locus>f.
                        3r</locus>)</incipit>
                     <note>A grammatical and allegorical commentary on the Pentateuch.</note>
                     <note>Autograph manuscript; the author mentions having begun writing the
                        commentary on the 10th of Tevet 5229 (<date calendar="Gregorian"
                        >1468</date>) (<locus>f. 3v</locus>), and having finished in 5230 (<locus>f.
                           212r</locus>).</note>
                     <note>Some of the <hi rend="italic">parashiyot</hi> have unusual names; for
                        example, <hi rend="italic">Parashat Avraham <foreign xml:lang="he">פרשת
                              אברהם</foreign></hi> for <hi rend="italic">Lekh Lekha</hi> and <hi
                           rend="italic">Parashat Memra <foreign xml:lang="he">פרשת
                           ממרא</foreign></hi> for <hi rend="italic">Vayera</hi>.</note>
                     <note>Marginal notes by <persName>Yeḳutiel <foreign xml:lang="he"
                              >יקותיאל</foreign></persName>, the author's grandson (he identifies
                        himself as such on <locus>f. 124v</locus>).</note>
                     <textLang mainLang="he">Hebrew</textLang>
                  </msItem>
                  <msItem n="2">
                     <locus>ff. 212v-220v</locus>
                     <title type="desc">Poetic works</title>
                     <author key="person_7038">
                        <persName>Elijah ben Samuel ben Parnes of Stephanow</persName>
                        <persName xml:lang="he">אליהו בן שמואל השטיפיוני</persName>
                     </author>
                     <note>Autograph manuscript.</note>
                     <note>Includes both liturgical and secular poems. The liturgical poems are
                        listed in Zunz, <hi rend="italic">Literaturgeschichte der synagogalen
                           Poesie</hi>, pp. 387-8, and edited by <persName>Abraham Meir
                           Habermanm</persName> in <hi rend="italic">Sefer ha-Yovel le-Rabi Ḥanokh
                           Albeḳ</hi>, pp. 160-76.</note>
                     <note>Several of the poems contains acrostics of the name of the author
                           (<persName>Eliah <foreign xml:lang="he">אליה</foreign></persName>,
                           <persName>Eliahu <foreign xml:lang="he">אליהו</foreign></persName> or
                           <persName>Eliah ha-Ḳaṭon <foreign xml:lang="he">אליה
                           הקטן</foreign></persName>), his father (<persName>Shmuʾel <foreign
                              xml:lang="he">שמואל</foreign></persName>), his brother-in-law
                           (<persName>Avraham <foreign xml:lang="he">אברהם</foreign></persName>),
                        his grandfather (<persName>Parnes <foreign xml:lang="he"
                           >פרנס</foreign></persName>) as well as the name <persName>Mosheh <foreign
                              xml:lang="he">משה</foreign></persName>, in a poem written for
                           <persName key="person_7090">Moses Capsali <foreign xml:lang="he">משה
                              המכונה קפשלי</foreign></persName>.</note>
                     <note>The collection also includes a short treatise on the calendar (<locus>ff.
                           218r-220r</locus>), which was composed in <date calendar="Gregorian"
                           >1468</date>.</note>
                     <textLang mainLang="he">Hebrew</textLang>

                  </msItem>
               </msContents>
               <physDesc>
                  <objectDesc form="codex">
                     <supportDesc>
                        <support>mixed (paper; outer sheets of quires made of parchment)</support>
                        <extent>ff. 220 <dimensions type="leaf" unit="mm">
                              <height>185</height>
                              <width>135</width>
                           </dimensions></extent>
                        <condition><locus>Ff. 1-2</locus> are deeply stained. The text on <locus>ff.
                              1v-2r</locus> has been deliberately scraped off and is now
                           illegible.</condition>
                     </supportDesc>
                  </objectDesc>
                  <handDesc>
                     <summary>Byzantine semi-cursive script</summary>
                     <handNote/>
                  </handDesc>
                  <decoDesc>
                     <p>Abstract monochrome penwork decoration around the incipit on <locus>f.
                           3r</locus>.</p>
                  </decoDesc>
                  <additions>
                     <p>On <locus>f. 2v</locus>, a liturgical poem for Shabbat evening which
                        contains the acrostic for '<persName>Eliahu ha-Ḳaṭon <foreign xml:lang="he"
                              >אליהו הקטן</foreign></persName>'. Even though the handwriting is
                        larger and more informal than the rest of the manuscript, it seems likely
                        that the poem was also copied by <persName>Elijah ben Samuel</persName>
                        himself, perhaps at a later time.</p>
                  </additions>
                  <bindingDesc>
                     <p>Vellum on paper boards; the spine bears the former shelfmark '278' (printed
                        on slip of paper which covers some writing in ink) and the current shelfmark
                        '218' (painted in black). </p>
                  </bindingDesc>
               </physDesc>
